<article class="env-lemma" id="01NH">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01NH">Lemma <span data-tag="01NH">27.13.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. The structure morphism $\mathbf{P}^ n_ S \to S$ is </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
separated, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
quasi-compact, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
satisfies the existence and uniqueness parts of the valuative criterion, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
universally closed. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      All these properties are stable under base change (this is clear for the last two and for the other two see Schemes, Lemmas <a href="/tag/01KU" data-tag="01KU">26.21.12</a> and <a href="/tag/01K5" data-tag="01K5">26.19.3</a>). Hence it suffices to prove them for the morphism $\mathbf{P}^ n_{\mathbf{Z}} \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Z})$. Separatedness is Lemma <a href="/tag/01MC" data-tag="01MC">27.8.8</a>. Quasi-compactness follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/01NG" data-tag="01NG">27.13.3</a>. Existence and uniqueness of the valuative criterion follow from Lemma <a href="/tag/01MF" data-tag="01MF">27.8.11</a>. Universally closed follows from the above and Schemes, Proposition <a href="/tag/01KF" data-tag="01KF">26.20.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>